WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.083
DNS maps a service hostname to an endpoint address.

00:00:03.083 --> 00:00:06.467
A recursive resolver can reuse an answer from its
cache.

00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:10.819
If it does not have one, it obtains an answer
through the DNS hierarchy.

00:00:10.819 --> 00:00:16.380
A global routing policy may return different
regional addresses, after which a regional load

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.952
balancer chooses a worker.

00:00:17.952 --> 00:00:19.837
Consider a simplified example.

00:00:19.837 --> 00:00:24.489
At time zero, a resolver caches region A with a
sixty second time to live.

00:00:24.489 --> 00:00:28.323
At ten seconds, the authoritative answer changes to
region B.

00:00:28.323 --> 00:00:31.403
Predict the result of a lookup at thirty seconds.

00:00:31.403 --> 00:00:35.866
The resolver still returns A, because its cached
entry has not expired.

00:00:35.885 --> 00:00:41.457
At sixty seconds, the next lookup in this model
refreshes the expired entry and discovers B.

00:00:41.457 --> 00:00:47.089
Real failover has additional delays: detecting the
failure, application caching, and existing

00:00:47.089 --> 00:00:47.816
connections.

00:00:47.816 --> 00:00:53.509
A lower time to live allows more frequent refreshes,
but creates more DNS traffic and does not

00:00:53.509 --> 00:00:55.023
promise instant recovery.

00:00:55.023 --> 00:01:00.881
Geographic DNS can use the requesting resolver’s
location, or sometimes partial client network

00:01:00.881 --> 00:01:02.875
information, to choose a region.

00:01:02.875 --> 00:01:06.614
That estimate may differ from the student’s actual
location.

00:01:06.614 --> 00:01:12.036
Global distribution also needs regional spare
capacity and a data replication strategy.

00:01:12.036 --> 00:01:17.021
DNS changes the endpoint a client discovers; it does
not replicate the database.
